- IHT
  Intermittent Hypoxia Normoxia

Intermittierende Hypoxie Hyperoxie Therapie, kurz IHHT ist ein verfahren bei dem
Im wechesl viel und wenig Sauersoff über eine Atemmaske zur verfügung gestellt wird. 

Der ReOxy ist im Grunde ein umgedrehter Sauerstoffkonzentrator zur durchführung
einer IHHT.

Die 3 Säulen der Wirkmechanismen


*** WIP Mechanism

How does IHHT/IHT Work

To give a better ovwerview of the Mechanism and the benefits we have to analyze
the Therapy on several levels. As a rule of Thumb we can say that the further
we go down the list the less is known and the more abstract it gets.

In Order to give an Overview of the clinical benefits and Mechanisms involved
and I will look in depth at two Reviews

[cite:@behrendtEffectsIntermittentHypoxia2022] Behrendt, Bielitzki & Behrens et al. (2022) Effects of Intermittent Hypoxia–Hyperoxia on Performance- and Health-Related Outcomes in Humans: A Systematic Review, Sports Medicine - Open.
Is outlining the Clinical Evidence and focuses on the functional impact of IHHT 

[cite:@burtscherMechanismsUnderlyingHealth2023] Burtscher, Citherlet & Camacho-Cardenosa et al. (2023) Mechanisms Underlying the Health Benefits of Intermittent Hypoxia Conditioning, The Journal of Physiology.
Focuses on the Mechanisms and potential Health Benefits of IHT/IHHT 

**** WIP Glossary 

The Reviews and Supporting papers urge for a consistent Terminology.[cite:@behrendtEffectsIntermittentHypoxia2022; @panzaIntermittentHypoxiaCall2023; @burtscherMechanismsUnderlyingHealth2023] 

The reasons for this are the following

- Hypoxia Normoxia are relative Terms
- Terminology has unclear definitions
- Different Terminologt between Diffrent Fields (Sleep Apnea, Intermitten Hypoxia, Cancer Research)
- Dose Dependency of The benefit vs detrimental effects
- Knowledge Gap about the dose contributions to diffrent Mechanisms

  In order to give a clearer picture we will define what we talk about accorting recommendations from 2023

#+begin_quote
Hypoxia
A condition in which oxygen supply does not match oxygen demand

Hypoxaemia
A partial pressure of oxygen in the arterial blood below 60 mmHg (8 kPa) or haemoglobin
oxygen saturation below 90%

Hyperoxia
elevated oxygen tension

Acute hypoxia
Initial phase of hypoxia exposure with most pronounced (patho)physiological responses

Chronic hypoxia
Prolonged (continuous or intermittent) hypoxia (days to years)

Functional hypoxia
Physical exercise or extensive motor-cognitive exercise

Continuous hypoxia
Exposure of an organism to uninterrupted hypoxia, as occurring during high-altitude

Intermittent hypoxia
(1) high-frequency intermittent hypoxia, e.g. obstructive sleep
apnoea, elicits mostly maladaptive responses;
(2) low-frequency intermittent hypoxia, e.g. intermittent hypoxia
exposure/conditioning, elicits mostly adaptive
responses.

Intermittent hypoxia conditioning, acute intermittent hypoxia, intermittent hypoxia training
Also termed IHE (see below), acute
intermittent hypoxia (this term is mainly used in studies on the potential of intermittent
hypoxia to modulate respiratory plasticity and/or if subjects with spinal cord injury are
exposed to intermittent hypoxia)

Intermittent hypoxia exposure/conditioning (IHE/IHC) versus intermittent hypoxia hyperoxic exposure/conditioning (IHHE/IHHC)
repeated 4–8 min exposures to hypoxia, i.e. fractions of
inspired oxygen (FIO2 = 10–15%, alternating with 2–5 min exposures to normoxia (IHE:
FIO2 = 21%) or hyperoxia (IHHE: FIO2 = 30–35%), totalling 35–40 min/session applied 3–5
times per week for 3–8 weeks. IHHE has also been termed intermittent hypoxia
hyperoxic conditioning (IHHC)

IHHT/IHT
same as IHHE/IHE T stands either for treatment or Therapy

AIH Acute Intermittent Hypoxia
Basically another term for IHE/IHC/IHT

#+end_quote


