**** WIP System Level Effects

#+begin_comment
System Level effects show direct and long term responses to hypoxia that are measurable
by equipment often Present in a doctors office. Like a Spirometer or an ECG
#+end_comment

  The Main Resource from here on out is the Review on Mechanisms
  from Burtscher et al.[cite:@burtscherMechanismsUnderlyingHealth2023]

#+begin_comment
Basically the Body senses low O2 and sends a signal to the Breathing center which in turn
tells the body to compensate for the lack of oxygen and sends the signals down the spine
to the breathing muscles and the heart.
#+end_comment

  Wether and how central Chemoreceptors direcly modulate hypoxia responses
  in humans is still debated. Classically chemoreceptors respond to pH and CO2.
  The main nuclei involved are the brain-stem pre-Bötzinger complex, retrotrapezius
  nucleus and raphe magnus nucleus.

[[file:d:/kroener/OneDrive - Kröner Medizintechnik/TMI/ReOxy/09_Projects/ReOxy_Guide/ReOxy_Guide.org_20240510_143117_lWrMjU.png]]  
  
  Contrary to the classic model, In a Hypoxic state there also a chemoreflex respons.
  It is assumed that this works through our Peripheral Chemoreceprors sensing low oxygen
  and or lactate and send a signal to the brain stem this induces Serotonin leading to
  a Neuro mediated Hypoxic Ventilatory Response and increased Cardiac output,
  additionally it can have positive effects on the Nervous system.
  
  The molecular mechanisms(per ex Mitochondria and HIF) colaborate with and enable this
  systemic oxygen sensing Leading to the hypoxic ventilatory response (HVR) instantiated
  by chemoreceprors particularly the cartoid bodies.
  Neurons transmit sensory impulses to activate the nervous system mediating the acute
  ventilatory chemoreflex response.


  It seems like the Cardiac and Ventilatory response to Intermittent Hypoxia
  accumulates when repeated and may benefit from additional task specific
  Training in certain modalities.

[[file:d:/kroener/OneDrive - Kröner Medizintechnik/TMI/ReOxy/09_Projects/ReOxy_Guide/ReOxy_Guide.org_20240510_141341_y1u4PW.png]] 

 

  The Cardiac and Ventilatory responses of Intermittent Hypoxia are Neuromediated
  Therefore we will start with the Nervous system.
 
***** GEPRÜFT Nervous System

Our Brain works with frequences. As a processor on a PC Hypoxia seems to influence
those frequiencies and interacts with our Nervous System.

We can observe some indications per ex. in increased maximum handgrip or in the
Improvement of Heart Rate Variability

Current Model:

AIH(IHE) induced motor plasticity is based largely on models of respiratory
Motor plasticity

Hypoxia -> Cartoid Body -> Respiratory Center -> Serotonin -> BDNF

- Q pathway triggered by mild AIH
- S pathway triggered by servere AIH and is dangerous for humans (were not doing that with IHHT)
https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qdOPGbdgYKk

#+CAPTION: In panel a: Neural network model of tAIH-induced plasticity, including hypoglossal (XII), phrenic and limb motor neurons (MN).
#+CAPTION: In panel b: Working model of cellular mechanisms giving rise to tAIH-induced motor plasticity. Moderate acute intermittent hypoxia (Po2 >40 mmHg) activates carotid body chemo-afferent neurons that then activate raphe serotonergic neurons and trigger serotonin release in widespread motor nuclei. Serotonin activates Gq coupled 5-HT receptors, initiating phrenic motor facilitation via the Q pathway (serotonin-induced, BDNF protein synthesis). Spinal tissue hypoxia also stimulates glia to release of ATP/adenosine into the extracellular space; subsequent binding to Gs coupled adenosine 2A receptors on motor neurons elicits a distinct intracellular signaling cascade that either: 1) elicits a distinct form of phrenic motor plasticity (but only at severe levels of tissue hypoxia), and/or 2) inhibits the Q pathway via cross-talk inhibition.
#+CAPTION: In panel c: tAIH effects are widespread among motor neuron pools, leading to plasticity of many motor systems, including: tongue/upper airway (XII), breathing (C3-C5), grasping (C6-T1) and locomotion (L4-S4). Thus, tAIH elicits global effects on motor neurons that elicit outcomes depending on the target muscle. The impact of AIH is enhanced by combination with task specific training, amplifying BDNF/TrkB effects within the targeted (task specific) motor system.
