****** Mitochondria

From the Review.

Prolonged continuous hypoxia may decrease mitochondrial mass by reducing
mitochondrial biogenesis, increasing mitophagy and altering mitochondrial
morphology and dynamics. Effects that have not been well characterized for IH.

Hypoxia/reoxygenation through aberrant fusion can produce ‘donut-shaped’
mitochondria (Liu & Hajnoczky, 2011) or megamitochondria, morphologies
potentially contributing to functional recovery or auto-phagic regulation,
respectively.

The remarkable plasticity of mitochondria (oxidative phosphorylation efficiency,
substrate utilization flexibility and reliance on alternative metabolic pathways) to
hypoxia and other stressors renders them primary candidates to mediate
beneficial hypoxia conditioning effects.

Improved mitochondrial quality control by
upregulated mitophagy and enhanced anti-oxidative mechanisms likely
contributes to improved cellular resilience effected by hypoxia conditioning,
but empirical evidence regarding the hypoxic doses that elicit these
protective effects is scarce, particularly for IH.

